{"causes": ["The Visigothic defeat and execution of the Suevic king Rechiar in 456 CE created a power vacuum and leadership crisis within the Suevic kingdom.", "Internal divisions among the Suevic nobility, fueled by rivalries and differing responses to Visigothic pressure, led to the election of Maldras as a contested leader.", "The ongoing collapse of Roman imperial authority in Hispania allowed various Germanic groups, including the Suevi, to establish and contest independent kingdoms."], "effects": ["Maldras's ascension cemented the political fragmentation of the Suevic Kingdom, leading to a civil war with rival claimants like Framta.", "His reign contributed to increased Suevic raiding against Roman and Visigothic territories in Hispania, destabilizing the region further.", "The internal strife initiated during Maldras's rule weakened the Suevic ability to resist future Visigothic expansion and ultimately prolonged their subjugation."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "rechiar", "sourceName": "Rechiar", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "maldras", "targetName": "Maldras", "context": "Maldras was elected king of the Suevi in 456 CE after Rechiar's defeat and execution by the Visigoths."}, {"sourceSlug": "theodoric-ii", "sourceName": "Theodoric II", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "maldras", "targetName": "Maldras", "context": "The Visigothic King Theodoric II's campaign and execution of Rechiar in 456 CE directly precipitated the Suevic election of Maldras."}, {"sourceSlug": "maldras", "sourceName": "Maldras", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "suevic-civil-war", "targetName": "Suevic Civil War", "context": "Maldras's reign was immediately marked by a civil war against a rival Suevic faction that elected Framta as king in 457 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "maldras", "sourceName": "Maldras", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "gallaecia", "targetName": "Gallaecia", "context": "Maldras ruled as king of the Suevic Kingdom, which was primarily centered in the Roman province of Gallaecia in northwestern Iberia."}, {"sourceSlug": "maldras", "sourceName": "Maldras", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "suevic-fragmentation", "targetName": "Suevic Fragmentation", "context": "Maldras's contested rule and the subsequent internal strife significantly exacerbated the fragmentation of the Suevic nation in the mid-5th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Braga, Portugal", "role": "Suevic Royal Seat"}, {"name": "Gallaecia, Roman Hispania", "role": "Kingdom's Territory"}, {"name": "Lusitania, Roman Hispania", "role": "Region of Raids"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Maldras", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "maldras"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2292792", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maldras"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Maldras's brief and contested reign solidified the internal fragmentation of the Suevic Kingdom, directly contributing to a period of civil war and weakening their ability to resist Visigothic expansion in Hispania.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-23T14:50:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30017546014", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Maldras was the Suevic king of Galicia from 456 until his death. After the execution of Rechiar by the victorious Visigoths, the Suevi are said to have established Maldras on the throne. During his reign the Suevic nation became fragmented.", "newValue": "Maldras, a Suevic military leader, ascended to the throne of the Suevic Kingdom of Gallaecia in 456 CE, ruling until his assassination in 460 CE. His reign is a critical, albeit brief, period in the post-Roman history of the Iberian Peninsula, marking a significant fragmentation of Suevic power following the Visigothic victory over King Rechiar. He represents a pivotal moment when the Suevi, a Germanic people who had established an independent kingdom in northwestern Iberia, struggled to maintain cohesion amidst external pressures from the Visigoths and internal dynastic rivalries, profoundly impacting the political landscape of Gallaecia.

After the execution of the powerful Suevic king Rechiar by the Visigothic king Theodoric II in 456 CE, the Suevic nobility, rather than submitting to Visigothic suzerainty, elected Maldras as their new monarch. This act of defiance occurred amidst the power vacuum created by Rechiar's death and the Visigothic campaigns. Maldras's rule was characterized by intense internal strife, notably a split within the Suevic nation, with a rival faction electing Framta as king in 457 CE. Maldras engaged in raids against Lusitania and the Roman province of Tarraconensis, often in alliance with Bagaudae, demonstrating a continued belligerent stance against Roman authority and Visigothic influence.

Maldras's reign solidified the fragmentation of the Suevic Kingdom, leading to a protracted period of civil war and weakening the Suevic ability to resist Visigothic expansion. His death in 460 CE, likely at the hands of his own people or a rival faction, did not resolve the internal divisions; instead, it ushered in a period where multiple Suevic claimants vied for power, such as Richimund and Frumarius. This internal instability ultimately facilitated the eventual subjugation of the Suevi by the Visigoths, even though their kingdom persisted for another century, albeit in a diminished and often client state capacity."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-23T14:50:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30017546014", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The Visigothic defeat and execution of the Suevic king Rechiar in 456 CE created a power vacuum and leadership crisis within the Suevic kingdom.", "Internal divisions among the Suevic nobility, fueled by rivalries and differing responses to Visigothic pressure, led to the election of Maldras as a contested leader.", "The ongoing collapse of Roman imperial authority in Hispania allowed various Germanic groups, including the Suevi, to establish and contest independent kingdoms."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-23T14:50:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30017546014", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "460-02-01"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "460-02-01"}], "newValue": ["Maldras's ascension cemented the political fragmentation of the Suevic Kin